Janis Papanagnou wrote:
> It's an opinion based on some design facts in the game.

And on a great deal of experience playing, testing, and designing games
as well.  This isn't exactly something I came up with off the cuff; I
made a point of finishing my 13 one-per-role ascensions on NAO (public
server, public record, etc.) before really solidly firming up my
opinions on some things.

(Mostly just so I knew I had the mechanics of the game itself down, but
for other reasons as well -- it's hard to speak coherently about optimal
Astral Plane strategy when you've only been through it once or twice,
for example.)

> Whether it is considered a flaw or not by the Devteam
> will show us the next Nethack version. 

More accurately, whether they considered it worth fixing.

Any change creates balance issues, and the Dev Team is not a single
person.  If they feel that a major fix would disrupt the game, then we
might only get a minor fix.  If they feel that even a minor fix would
disrupt the game sufficiently, then nothing might happen, regardless of
whether they think it's a flaw or not.  Of course, if they think it's a
flaw, it'll be far more likely to at least get _some_ change.

Just because something is broken doesn't mean it'll get fixed right
away.  Nethack is a large, ponderous beast, and sometimes changing
things in one corner can break things in another corner; the nature of
the wide variety of interactions possible in the game is such that
changes must be approached with caution.

(Admittedly, I personally don't see anything to be overly cautious of in
this case, and I expect testing will prove it out to some degree on my
own server, but there's still value in mentioning the above.)

